Consider the following C code used to verify an HMAC‑SHA256 signature on incoming API requests:

```c
bool verify_request(const char *provided_sig, const char *msg, const unsigned char *key) {
    unsigned char computed_sig[32];
    HMAC(EVP_sha256(), key, KEY_LEN, (unsigned char *)msg, strlen(msg), computed_sig, NULL);
    char hex_sig[65];
    for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) sprintf(hex_sig + i*2, "%02x", computed_sig[i]);
    if (strcmp(provided_sig, hex_sig) == 0) {
        return true; // accept request
    }
    return false; // reject request
}
```
Which subtle flaw makes this verification vulnerable, and which mitigation directly addresses it?

